The cortisol patterns in children were divided into three groups: hypercortisol (68%), characterized by a persistent elevation in cortisol; hypocortisol (9%), characterized by a persistent decrease in cortisol; and hypercortisol-to-deficit cortisol (23%) where cortisol levels start high and then decrease to normal levels. Children in the hypercortisol-to-deficit cortisol group experienced fewer mental health issues (both internal and external symptoms) compared to those in the hypercortisol group. Children in the hypocortisol group showed no significant difference in psychological symptoms compared to healthy children in the hypercortisol-to-deficit group.
